<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gadag</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gadag is a city in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, which with neighbouring Betageri forms a twin city. It was an important religious and artistic centre under the Western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalukya-dynasty">Chalukyas</a> in the 11th and 12th centuries, when the richly carved Trikuteshwara and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saraswati">Saraswati</a> temples were built. It is also known for the Veeranarayana temple, associated with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannada">Kannada</a> poet Kumaravyasa.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gadag.jpg" alt="Gadag" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Vinayak Kulkarni (Bangalore) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Veera_Narayana_Temple,_Gadag.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
